#%PAM-1.0
#
# Login through bsm-helper (root), for Login and Switch authentications.
#
# bsm-helper runs pam_authenticate and pam_acct_mgmt for the conversation, keeps
# that PAM handle in the authenticated child and, when the ticket is redeemed,
# runs pam_setcred and pam_open_session on the SAME handle. So the session phase
# below sees what the auth phase learnt (pam_gnome_keyring keeps the password
# for auto_start, Kerberos and sssd credentials follow the authentication).
# The session stays open for the whole life of the hosted desktop and is closed
# only when the desktop ends.
#
# pam_systemd registers a logind session WITHOUT a seat: the helper puts
# XDG_SESSION_TYPE=wayland and XDG_SESSION_CLASS=user in the PAM environment and
# never sets XDG_SEAT or XDG_VTNR, because bsm owns seat0 (docs/bsm/architecture.md,
# D4). Fedora's system-auth already carries an optional pam_systemd; a second
# pam_systemd.so in the same PAM handle returns early without creating another
# session. The helper checks that the session scope exists and fails the login
# otherwise.
#
# `substack`, not `include` (review F8): a `sufficient` module in system-auth (pam_unix,
# pam_fprintd) ends only the substack, so the lines after it still run. With
# `include` a successful `sufficient` pam_unix ended the whole auth stack and
# pam_gnome_keyring never saw the password.
#
# Lockout (pam_faillock), password policy and fingerprint (pam_fprintd) come
# from system-auth, so they are exactly the machine's own policy and bsm adds
# none. A fingerprint prompt reaches the greeter as an info message; the
# fingerprint module and the password prompt are answered through the same
# conversation.
auth      substack  system-auth
auth      optional  pam_gnome_keyring.so
account   required  pam_nologin.so
account   substack  system-auth
password  include   system-auth
session   optional  pam_keyinit.so force revoke
session   required  pam_loginuid.so
session   substack  system-auth
session   required  pam_systemd.so
session   optional  pam_gnome_keyring.so auto_start
